ltenham,georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>A vicious cycle of debt and po=
pulism</span></h2><p style=3D"display:block;color:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px=
 georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>By Eshe Nelson and Jim Tankersley</sp=
an></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 =
0 15px"><span>When protests over high gas prices ripped through France in 2=
018, President Emmanuel Macron offered about $11 billion in tax cuts and wa=
ge increases to try to quell the unrest.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal =
17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>He can=E2=80=99t =
do that this time around.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georg=
ia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Today, protests that initially b=
egan over education have turned violent. But Macron is no longer in a posit=
ion to throw money at the problem.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/2=
5px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That=E2=80=99s because =
the French government is also </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/ne=
wsletter/w8qQmcIGk825AUPNGKzwFQ~~/AAAAARA~/QkfhhirVSRTd6nfNud0MlbXUtsElfI5n=
R68P7NC4OQanMwKiQ2182s-C60DVdMVe88URMLNtEClnCnuB9YeFxe5nwrJkW89nNn9RjA4zNj6=
E9DX9XIZyB-awDgVY3ugQY1l0zMG9NK79pbZuEyelruUHnM8O441pi00YFSrXMoRq8ctVmdpxB_=
nbRWjZO8LeK_DvB6bl3BTcnSQiN77ua70g-16ak0xRAGFdhrhGCGoUU3CzJXvDXRtuiq8bcroZs=
z0BNglIksTHCPmn6k0miX-uQYvm3UnFfcvQGkIKu-lf6_LEwHRnhie1y4CLvkP-A116gbSLMC7J=
rjcUHrpJYXNazmXWHMuRoROR7Q9sdrfoLNWlLDZnmS5Q8uZhZwbP" class=3D"css-sdwaa1" =
style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-decoration:un=
derline">fighting the prospect of a crisis in its bond market</a><span>. In=
vestors have been dumping French debt, unsure of the government=E2=80=99s a=
bility to manage its budget amid high interest rates and political uncertai=
nty; announcing a big financial commitment to mollify protesters would most=
 likely only rattle investors further.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Macron, in other wo=
rds, is in a tight spot. Do nothing, and the protests could continue. Spend=
 more, and he could ultimately leave France with even less money for areas =
like education, by making borrowing even more expensive. Both scenarios wou=
ld most likely add to the momentum of the populist politicians running for =
his seat next year.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,ser=
if;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Trapped between angry citizens and war=
y investors, Macron could wind up in an unhappy place =E2=80=94 what resear=
chers described to us as a populism-bond market =E2=80=9C</span><a href=3D"=
https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/9GuMkWg_ZwNPeJmZ3jDm5w~~/AAAAARA~/IsblL=
cpbrzWHTGSua3lPxFR1R3mphTBTVIDgc9hZv9Q9dhD-4BlsCMqd2JP1IKqnsjNLjOy61epJL72J=
ltPiGXyUHK-jm9J3wlUCJ8DBQ7cZDO7CYvDK4yvVJ-H8QwLPpoCgiUfKoj10V2RSZMgZgGIUdTN=
pR9iQfA9zFivDf6Cjp8RxnxX_21J0ZgLmrFDoVDxMRERacm32MrX-m7q6_os_QvrxufDD_MHI6z=
54QaaFTVn1-miTgrAJbQDBmxgAnzHgeTYY10IhoF7_lo3gxd0MTO-3e76h-URpDljseck5s4xvK=
TICK5l3EyOeM7TacdufySkAO0nPm5WSKNqpXvgyl6cxgjZ2nBUxXWCo18N6LrzbNm62n7w26ZvV=
KMU2" class=3D"css-sdwaa1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:u=
nderline;text-decoration:underline">doom loop.</a><span>=E2=80=9D</span></p=
><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px=
"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Voters vs. investors</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>F=
rance is not the only country facing a debt trap. As borrowing has grown in=
 many wealthy nations, including France, Britain and the United States, pop=
ulist politics are surging. The combination makes for a combustible mix.</s=
pan></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0=
 0 15px"><span>Market turmoil and political tumult can feed off each other,=
 according to Michal Brzozowski and Joanna Siwinska-Gorzelak, researchers a=
t the University of Warsaw in Poland, who published a paper on the relation=
ship between populism and fiscal policy.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal =
17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>At the root of th=
e problem is a considerable tension: Amid deep political and fiscal uncerta=
inty, voters and investors appear to want different things.</span></p><p st=
yle=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><spa=
n>Buyers of debt want to be adequately compensated for the risk of lending =
to governments. When they see governments unable to make difficult choices,=
 such as cutting spending or increasing taxes, to reduce yawning budget def=
icits, what they see is a loan that=E2=80=99s getting riskier. As a result,=
 they demand higher interest payments.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Voters, though, are=
 increasingly </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/jfj1qhV=
Y1Oj4iPQs1m-czQ~~/AAAAARA~/sMrrmePlr7ZsdyrENC15ZhlNyDtfd91t9G6ufLLBproC_NmB=
THb0pQbOI1lDYvG_7nYjEqvicOsk0evxIgiPRUI4mFypw8KoIV-q-t4EXzNVG_AUTfvgKlI0Lnr=
6xRT7v9QLYmv7hV7otuQXaXQcXNIDyQCWcJ1mHA6qcwog8VzKJu2hwFPf1FDMAkQo8U-8BEhpCI=
uYuk9gwzkb3nyLxGmoVTnMmQpQt6O_VxXXs--ijf5GqnUuI3mMB2A-bGk-Ft9x4OLdnQAlYfqEv=
g_fwLCZQ5F06_Nui_YOyxyEaKPNvQPFLhqeroB4zoWUE9H0mmouNT2HesarCTSJD5UegQ1hws6_=
tZMpHi2X_no-i7Dqh5cGrRt6f3H4U0nXjfHaM0Qzg7Z9fMbZ4Ho2qz3l0w~~" class=3D"css-=
sdwaa1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-decor=
ation:underline">turning to populist parties</a><span> that largely reject =
the idea that budgets require these difficult choices. Instead, they promis=
e to protect generous retirement programs and other spending, while often p=
roposing to cut taxes, or to raise taxes only on the wealthy, which in most=
 cases wouldn=E2=80=99t go far enough.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The resulting tensi=
ons could spiral like this: Economically anxious voters turn to populists, =
who promise a pain-free return to better days, and buyers of government deb=
t recoil.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#=
333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Catch-22</span></p><p =
style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><s=
pan>Some centrist politicians today are still pledging fiscal discipline =
=E2=80=94 the kind of pledges that voters once rewarded. But today, such po=
licies tend to leave the public with the impression that those politicians =
